# Postgres connection string (local Postgres or Neon via Vercel Marketplace).
# The app boots and renders fine with this unset — DB access is lazy-init
# and only required once persistence-backed routes are hit.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@localhost:5432/emergent

# The real (non-mock) agent loop runs on the Claude Agent SDK, which wraps
# the local `claude` CLI and uses whatever the CLI is already logged in as
# (Claude Code subscription auth via `claude login`) — no API key needed for
# local dev. ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is NOT read by the agent loop and does not
# need to be set. Set MOCK_AGENT=1 to run the scripted mock trajectory
# instead (no model calls, no CLI required).
# MOCK_AGENT=1

# --- Phase 3, optional, both gated inert when unset -------------------------

# Clerk auth. Leave both unset (the default) and the app keeps running in
# single-user dev mode exactly as before — no Clerk code runs at all, not
# even at import time (see src/lib/auth.ts's isClerkConfigured(), src/
# proxy.ts, src/components/auth/ClerkGate.tsx). Set both to real values from
# a Clerk dashboard to replace DEV_USER with real signed-in users.
# CLERK_SECRET_KEY=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# GitHub "Save" (src/server/github-app.ts) — a real GitHub App +
# installation-token flow (matching Lovable's "Install on GitHub" pattern),
# not a static personal access token. Leave both unset (the default) and the
# Save button surfaces "GitHub not configured" instead of failing silently or
# crashing.
#
# Register a GitHub App at https://github.com/settings/apps/new with:
#   - Repository permissions: Contents (read/write), Administration
#     (read/write), Metadata (read-only).
#   - Webhooks: disabled (this app doesn't handle webhook events).
#   - Setup URL: http://localhost:3000/api/github/install/callback (must
#     match this app's callback route exactly).
#
# POST /orgs/{org}/repos (creating a repo in an org) is enabledForGitHubApps,
# but POST /user/repos (creating a repo on a personal account) is NOT — an
# installation token alone can never create a new repo on a personal
# account, org installs don't hit this at all. Reading/writing files in an
# EXISTING repo works the same regardless of account type. Two ways to
# handle personal accounts creating a brand-new repo:
#   1. (Simplest) Install the app on a GitHub ORGANIZATION instead.
#   2. Configure GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID/SECRET below so personal-account users
#      grant a user-to-server OAuth token during install — see that section.
# If neither applies, Save fails with a clear error telling the user to
# reinstall on an org (see GitHubPersonalAccountRepoCreationError in
# github-app.ts).
#
# GITHUB_APP_ID= the App's numeric App ID (shown on the App's settings page).
# GITHUB_APP_ID=

# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_BASE64= the App's private key (generate one on the
# App's settings page, downloads as a .pem file), base64-encoded onto a
# single line so it can live in a .env file without breaking on embedded
# newlines. Produce it with:
#   base64 -i path/to/your-app.private-key.pem | tr -d '\n'
# (on Linux, use `base64 -w0` instead of `-i ... | tr -d '\n'`). The app
# decodes this back to the real PEM string at runtime via
# Buffer.from(process.env.GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_BASE64, "base64").toString("utf8").
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_BASE64=

# GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID / GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET — optional. Lets users who
# install the App on their PERSONAL account (not an org) still create
# brand-new repos, by additionally granting a user-to-server OAuth token
# (the only token type GitHub allows for POST /user/repos). Leave both unset
# and personal-account users just get told to reinstall on an org instead —
# nothing breaks, this only unlocks the extra path.
#
# To enable: on the same GitHub App's settings page
# (https://github.com/settings/apps/your-app):
#   - Check "Request user authorization (OAuth) during installation".
#   - Set "Authorization callback URL" to the SAME URL as the Setup URL
#     above (http://localhost:3000/api/github/install/callback) — GitHub
#     then appends a `code` param alongside installation_id on that same
#     redirect, no separate consent screen or callback route needed.
#   - Copy the Client ID and generate a Client secret from that page.
# No new repository permission is needed — this only adds a second,
# independent token type on top of the existing installation token.
# GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Phase 4, optional, all gated inert when unset -------------------------

# Production credential source for the real agent loop (src/server/agent.ts's
# getAgentEnv()). Leave unset (the default) and the SDK's query() calls omit
# `env` entirely, so the subprocess inherits this process's shell and falls
# through to the local `claude` CLI's own login — identical to Phase 1-3
# behavior. Set this only in a deploy that holds a real, centrally-managed
# key (never a per-user/per-laptop credential — see PLAN.md's Phase 4 row).
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Vercel deploy ("Deploy Your Application" — src/server/vercel.ts). A
# personal or team access token from https://vercel.com/account/tokens.
# Leave unset (the default) and the Deploy button surfaces "Vercel deploy is
# not configured" instead of failing silently or crashing.
# VERCEL_TOKEN=
# Only needed if VERCEL_TOKEN is scoped to a team rather than a personal
# account — the team's id/slug, so deployments are created on its behalf.
# VERCEL_TEAM_ID=

# Stripe billing ("Buy Credits" — src/server/stripe.ts). Leave both unset
# (the default) and clicking "Buy Credits" surfaces "Stripe is not
# configured" instead of failing silently or crashing. Note: this
# implementation redirects to Stripe-hosted Checkout (session.url) rather
# than embedding Stripe.js/Elements client-side, so no publishable key is
# needed.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# Required for POST /api/webhooks/stripe to verify delivery signatures
# (stripe.webhooks.constructEvent) — without it, credit-pack purchases can
# never actually grant credits, even with STRIPE_SECRET_KEY set.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
